INT. PAWNSHOP - BACK OFFICE - NIGHT

A windowless room behind the counter. A steel desk. A wall of guitars. A space heater ticks under the table.

DETECTIVE MALLORY (50s, rumpled sport coat, tired eyes) sits in the visitor's chair with a paper cup of coffee. VITO (60s, pawnbroker, leather vest, rings on every finger) leans back behind the desk, hands folded on his stomach. CHLOE (20s, Vito's niece, apron over a band T-shirt) stands by the guitar wall, rearranging the necks of two acoustics, her back to the room.

MALLORY
Vito, I'm not here about the Fender.

VITO
Good. Because the Fender's clean.

MALLORY
I'm not here about the watches either.

VITO
The watches are also clean.

MALLORY
I'm here about Eddie Cruz.

VITO
Never heard of him.

MALLORY
He came in on the eleventh. Pawned a ring. Never came back.

VITO
People forget their rings.

MALLORY
Not Eddie. Eddie loved that ring. It was his late wife's.

Vito shrugs. Chloe turns a guitar peg, plinking a note.

MALLORY (CONT'D)
Chloe. Was it you on the counter that night?

CHLOE
I work most nights.

MALLORY
Then you saw him.

CHLOE
I see a lot of people.

VITO
Detective, the girl is nervous around police.

MALLORY
I've noticed.

He drinks his coffee. Sets it on the desk. Stretches his legs, comfortable, in no rush.

MALLORY (CONT'D)
You know, Vito, my mother had a pawnshop. Little place in Queens. Sold my father's trombone to pay for my First Communion.

VITO
Sad story.

MALLORY
It wasn't sad. He hated that trombone.

VITO
(smiling)
I like your mother.

MALLORY
She'd like you. She'd also count the change.

Vito laughs. Chloe even smiles. The tension in the room loosens. Mallory leans forward and picks up a pawn ticket from the desk, turns it over.

MALLORY (CONT'D)
Here's what I think. I think Eddie came in, you gave him two hundred dollars, and he went home. I think a week later, a man came in and bought that ring for four times what you paid.

VITO
That's business.

MALLORY
I think the man who bought it was the one Eddie owed money to. I think that man followed Eddie home. And I think when Eddie's body turns up in the canal, the ring will be on the other man's finger, and the other man will say he bought it at a pawnshop on Delancey.

VITO
(still smiling)
That's a story.

MALLORY
It's a good story. It needs a witness. It needs somebody who saw the man at the counter.

Chloe stops plinking. She turns, slowly, and Mallory finally looks at her.

CHLOE
Uncle Vito...

VITO
Chloe.

CHLOE
He's going to find out.

VITO
(sharp)
Chloe, go to the front.

CHLOE
I don't want to.

Vito stands. The ring on his finger glints. He reaches into his desk drawer, and from it draws a small, plain revolver. Mallory doesn't flinch.

MALLORY
Vito, put it down.

VITO
Not until the girl keeps quiet.

MALLORY
She's not the one you're afraid of.

VITO
Then who?

MALLORY
The man at the counter that night. The one who bought the ring. His name is Sergeant Dobbs. He's my partner. He's standing outside the back door, and he's very, very patient.

The back door opens. A cold draft. Vito's hand drops. Chloe lets out a sob. Mallory finishes his coffee in one swallow and rises.

MALLORY (CONT'D)
I told you I wasn't here about the Fender.
